\chapter{Deriving Visitors from AST
Schemas}\label{ch34-deriving-visitors-from-ast-schemas}

A visitor combines the vocabulary discovered in an AST schema with
user-supplied semantic handlers. Template instantiation generates
handler dispatch; the schema determines which tags are accepted.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Register operations with
\texttt{on}}{Register operations with }}\label{ch34-register-operations-with-on}

\texttt{on\textless{}\allowbreak{}"tag"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}}
in C++ and \texttt{on!"tag"(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}} in D produce
a \texttt{VisitorCase}. Handlers may take one node argument or a
compile-time tag followed by a node. The node carries runtime values;
the tag identifies the handler specialization.

\begin{code}
auto number = dsl::on<"num">([](auto tag, const dsl::ASTNode& node) {
    static_assert(tag.value == "num");
    assert(!node.value().empty());
});
\end{code}

\begin{code}
struct NumberHandler
{
    void opCall(Tag)(Tag tag, ref ASTNode node)
    {
        static assert(Tag.value == "num");
        assert(node.value().length != 0);
    }
}
auto number = on!"num"(NumberHandler());
\end{code}

D also accepts alias-bound generic handlers through
\texttt{on!(\allowbreak{}tag,\allowbreak{} fn)\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
Concrete functions/delegates/functors retain their types in the visitor.

\section{Derive from a runtime AST}\label{ch34-derive-from-a-runtime-ast}

\begin{code}
auto schema = dsl::node<"add">(dsl::leaf<"num">(0));
int sum = 0;
auto visitor = dsl::derive_visitor(schema,
    dsl::on<"add">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}),
    dsl::on<"num">([&](const dsl::ASTNode& n) { sum += std::stoi(n.value()); }));
auto tree = dsl::node<"add">(dsl::leaf<"num">(2), dsl::leaf<"num">(3));
visitor.walk(tree);
assert(sum == 5);
\end{code}

\begin{code}
import std.conv : to;
auto schema = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(0));
int sum;
auto visitor = derive_visitor(schema,
    on!"add"((ref ASTNode n) {}),
    on!"num"((ref ASTNode n) { sum += to!int(n.value()); }));
auto tree = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(2), leaf!"num"(3));
visitor.walk(tree);
assert(sum == 5);
\end{code}

Schema collection discovers unique tags recursively. Its values and
arities do not constrain later trees: the schema above permits an
\texttt{add} with two \texttt{num} children although its example tree
has only one. The validation contract is tag coverage. The user handler
can enforce additional structural rules.

Every schema tag must have a handler at construction. An unknown tag in
a visited tree raises an exception. Handlers execute in registration
order, with the first matching case used.
\texttt{compile\_\allowbreak{}visitor(\allowbreak{}runtimeSchema,\allowbreak{} handlers.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
also offers this runtime-validated construction.

\section{Validate the schema at
compilation}\label{ch34-validate-the-schema-at-compilation}

C++ supplies a structural tree of types:

\begin{code}
auto schema = dsl::ast_schema<"add">(dsl::ast_schema<"num">());
auto visitor = dsl::compile_visitor(schema,
    dsl::on<"add">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}),
    dsl::on<"num">([](const dsl::ASTNode&) {}));
\end{code}

\texttt{ASTSchema\textless{}\allowbreak{}Tag,\allowbreak{} Children.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
records the tag and child types. \texttt{ast\_\allowbreak{}schema}
constructs its type-level vocabulary, and
\texttt{compile\_\allowbreak{}visitor} checks handler coverage with a
compile-time assertion before creating the runtime visitor.

D can use an \texttt{ASTNode} value computed by CTFE:

\begin{code}
enum schema = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(0));
auto visitor = compile_visitor!schema(
    on!"add"((ref ASTNode n) {}),
    on!"num"((ref ASTNode n) {}));
\end{code}

Each discovered schema tag is checked by
\texttt{static foreach}/\texttt{static assert}. The handler code is
still supplied by the user. A runtime schema selects accepted tags and
validates them at runtime; a static schema validates them during
host-language compilation. Neither path performs runtime JIT
compilation.

\section{Dispatch and traversal}\label{ch34-dispatch-and-traversal}

\texttt{visit(\allowbreak{}node)\allowbreak{}} dispatches one node.
\texttt{walk(\allowbreak{}node,\allowbreak{} order)\allowbreak{}}
recursively traverses:

