Preorder is the default. C++ traversal supports const or mutable ASTs
when the handlers accept the corresponding node reference. D's public
visitor operations take \texttt{ref ASTNode}, permitting mutation by
callbacks. Handlers that change child structure affect the traversal as
it runs; choose the order to suit the transformation.

\section{Equality-triggered visitor
operations}\label{ch34-equality-triggered-visitor-operations}

After saturation, convert an extracted representative to
\texttt{ASTNode} and dispatch it through a visitor:

\begin{code}
auto graph = new EGraph;
auto root = graph.add("(add a 0)");
auto atom = graph.add("a");
assert(graph.saturate(sexpr_rules("(= (add ?x 0) ?x)")).saturated());
size_t calls;
graph.when_equal(root, atom, (EGraph g, EClassId id) {
    auto tree = g.extract_ast(id);
    enum schema = leaf!"atom"("");
    auto visitor = compile_visitor!schema(
        on!"atom"((ref ASTNode n) { ++calls; assert(n.value() == "a"); }));
    visitor.walk(tree);
});
assert(calls == 1);
\end{code}

The C++ equivalent is in
\href{../examples/26-equality-saturation.cpp}{\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}26-equality-satu\allowbreak{}ration.\allowbreak{}cpp}}.
Grammar-produced ASTs use nonterminal names and \texttt{token} leaves,
so the same visitor mechanism can implement grammar semantic passes
(Chapter 36).
